 "text": "You think everyone noticed. They didn't. In 2000, Cornell psychologists made students wear an embarrassing t-shirt with Barry Manilow's face on it. Then, each student walked late into a room full of people. Afterward, they guessed about half the room noticed the shirt. The real number? About a quarter. Here's the trick. It's called the spotlight effect. You're the main character in your head, but everyone else is the main character in theirs. They're too busy worrying about their own shirt. So that awkward thing you said? Fewer people noticed than you think. Follow for more mind tricks.",
